@page "/docs/tools/overview"

@* The section's first page: every tool, what it is for, where it runs and what it needs. The rows of "What each needs"
   repeat the pricing page's table (Components/Pages/Pricing.razor, Makes): keep the two in step. The VS Code extension's
   pages are written and held back until it is published (Docs/Drafts/VsCode; see Articles.cs): then add its row to The
   tools ("IzzyBlazor for VS Code", docs/tools/vs-code, "In VS Code") and to What each needs. Until Nov 26, 2026
   this section was "The izzy CLI" at docs/cli (redirected). *@

<ArticlePage Section="tools" Slug="overview">
    <Lead>
        IzzyBlazor's tools create apps and pages, keep an app up to date, check its accessibility and give a
        coding agent the facts about the library. Each one writes plain code into your project or reports on
        it. None of them is needed to use the components.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">The tools</Heading>
        <Table Items="Tools" Caption="IzzyBlazor's developer tools" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params site-params-prose site-params-stack" Bordered="false" SmallScreen="TableSmallScreen.Stack" StackBelow="Length.Rem(40)">
            <Columns>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Tool"><Anchor Href="@context!.Href">@context.Name</Anchor></TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="What it does">@context!.Text</TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Runs">@context!.Runs</TemplateColumn>
            </Columns>
        </Table>
        <TextBlock>
            All of them but the docs server are the <Code>izzy</Code> command, one .NET tool. To install it, see
            <Anchor Href="docs/tools/cli">The izzy CLI</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Pick a tool by task</Heading>
        <Table Items="Tasks" Caption="The tool for a task" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params site-params-prose site-params-stack" Bordered="false" SmallScreen="TableSmallScreen.Stack" StackBelow="Length.Rem(40)">
            <Columns>
                <TemplateColumn Title="To">@context!.Name</TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Run"><code>@context!.Runs</code></TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Read"><Anchor Href="@context!.Href">@context.Text</Anchor></TemplateColumn>
            </Columns>
        </Table>

        <Heading Level="2">What each needs</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            The components need no account and no tool. The tools work with no account too, and do more with a
            free account or with IzzyBlazor Pro. <Anchor Href="pricing">Pricing</Anchor> has the whole list.
        </TextBlock>
        <Table Items="Needs" Caption="What each tool does, by account" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params site-params-prose site-params-stack" Bordered="false" SmallScreen="TableSmallScreen.Stack" StackBelow="Length.Rem(40)">
            <Columns>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Tool">@context!.Name</TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="No account">@context!.Open</TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="A free account">@context!.Free</TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Pro">@context!.Pro</TemplateColumn>
            </Columns>
        </Table>
        <TextBlock>
            Sign in once on each machine with <Code>izzy login</Code>, and use a key on a build server: see
            <Anchor Href="docs/tools/account">Your account</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">What every tool keeps to</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>The code is yours. A tool adds plain Razor and C# files to your project, and nothing checks a license when the app builds or runs. See the <Anchor Href="docs/tools/license">License</Anchor>.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>You see a change first. Every command that changes your app's code takes <Code>--dry-run</Code> and <Code>--diff</Code>, and none replaces a file you've changed unless you pass <Code>--overwrite</Code>. <Code>izzy agent</Code> takes <Code>--dry-run</Code> and replaces its own skill file with the current one. See <Anchor Href="docs/tools/changes">Previewing changes</Anchor>.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>Your code stays on your machine. izzy creates code locally, online or offline, and never sends your code or your app's name. A sign-in sends the machine's name, its operating system and izzy's version.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>A person and an agent get the same result. <Code>izzy mcp</Code> runs the commands you run in a terminal.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>The audit reports and changes no code, and a report with no findings is not a statement of conformance. See <Anchor Href="docs/accessibility/automated-and-manual">Automated and manual checks</Anchor>.</ListItem>
        </List>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>

